import{_ as t,o,c as a,a0 as c}from"./chunks/framework.BLL-IG04.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Explanation: claims, tiers, and the evidence bar","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/claims.md","filePath":"explanation/claims.md"}'),d={name:"explanation/claims.md"};function i(s,e,n,r,l,m){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[c('<h1 id="explanation-claims-tiers-and-the-evidence-bar" tabindex="-1">Explanation: claims, tiers, and the evidence bar <a class="header-anchor" href="#explanation-claims-tiers-and-the-evidence-bar" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Explanation: claims, tiers, and the evidence bar&quot;">​</a></h1><p>mimus is built on one observation: documentation is full of <em>claims</em> — statements that are either still true or quietly false. A critic&#39;s job is to enumerate them and prove each one, or stay silent.</p><h2 id="claim-taxonomy" tabindex="-1">Claim taxonomy <a class="header-anchor" href="#claim-taxonomy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Claim taxonomy&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Extraction classifies backticked spans, code blocks and prose patterns into:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>kind</th><th>example claim</th><th>verified by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>path</code></td><td><code>`src/lib.rs`</code></td><td>filesystem</td></tr><tr><td><code>command</code></td><td><code>`cargo test -p foo`</code></td><td>binary on PATH (no exec)</td></tr><tr><td><code>symbol</code></td><td><code>`IndexStore`</code></td><td>kuda index (symbol checker)</td></tr><tr><td><code>count</code></td><td>&quot;357 suites / 4,298 tests&quot;</td><td>recomputation</td></tr><tr><td><code>link</code></td><td><code>[docs](https://…)</code></td><td>link checker</td></tr><tr><td><code>behavior</code></td><td>prose asserting code does X</td><td>rules + semantic tier</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Command claims are conservative by design: only spans whose first token is a known binary (<code>cargo</code>, <code>git</code>, <code>npm</code>, <code>dagger</code>, <code>kuda</code>, <code>mimus</code>, …) classify as commands — otherwise <code>pub fn</code> and <code>data Eq</code> get read as shell.</p><h2 id="verification-tiers" tabindex="-1">Verification tiers <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification-tiers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification tiers&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Mechanical</strong> — deterministic and offline: paths, commands, rules. This is the <code>mimus check</code> gate; if it flags something, the evidence is reproducible by anyone.</li><li><strong>Graph</strong> (ADR-0002 — all six classes live) — set-level pass, not per-claim: claims and kuda-index edges become <code>(subject, relation, object)</code> triples bucketed by subject. Same bucket, different object → <code>contradiction</code> (doc↔doc) or <code>self-contradiction</code> (reflexive), warn-tier — index-free, runs on every claim set; index edge with no target → <code>stale-reference</code> (error tier); prose edge the index lacks → <code>unsupported claim</code>; index module no document names → <code>undocumented fact</code> (warn, see below). <code>[check].aliases</code> folds differently-spelled subjects into one bucket. A per-file triples cache (<code>.mimus/triples.db</code>, gitignored) skips re-extracting unchanged files, and under <code>--diff</code> every graph finding is gated to sources the run actually looked at. The mechanical pass is also the semantic tier&#39;s <em>intake filter</em>: what the buckets can&#39;t settle (near-duplicate subjects — canon-differing but sharing a token — asserting different objects for one relation) becomes a pre-bucketed candidate pair the agent adjudicates, so it never scans every claim. Mechanical speed — buckets, not agents.</li></ul><h3 id="undocumented-fact-vs-mimus-coverage" tabindex="-1"><code>undocumented fact</code> vs <code>mimus coverage</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#undocumented-fact-vs-mimus-coverage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`undocumented fact` vs `mimus coverage`&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Both run code→doc, but they answer different questions and sit on different tiers:</p><ul><li><code>mimus coverage</code> (command, advisory, symbol level) lists <em>symbols</em> the index holds and no doc source ever names — a to-do list for writers, never part of <code>check</code>.</li><li><code>undocumented fact</code> (graph class, warn, module level) flags a <strong>public module</strong> — one the index records as a dependency target of another module — that no document in the mined set names. It rides the <code>check</code> pipeline (gated by <code>[check].kuda_index</code>, scoped by <code>--diff</code> on the module&#39;s own files) because a missing module page is a contract gap, not a per-symbol chore.</li></ul><p>Both match names canonically: <code>mimus-core</code>, <code>mimus_core</code> and any <code>[check].aliases</code> spelling count. The fact class is deliberately coarse — every indexed import is a fact nobody documents, so edge-level reverse coverage would flag the whole repo; a module is the smallest unit a doc page can cover.</p><ul><li><strong>Semantic</strong> — advisory: the <code>acp</code> checker spawns an ACP agent (<code>acp_cmd</code>, e.g. <code>env CLINE_PROVIDER=cline-pass CLINE_MODEL=cline-free/mimo-v2.6-flash cline --acp</code> — provider/model are env vars; -P/-m flags are ignored in ACP mode) to verify <code>behavior</code> claims — including kuda card prose. Runs with <code>--all</code> or <code>[check].semantic</code>; every finding is <code>warn</code> + evidence. <strong>Can never gate</strong> — that is load-bearing, not a limitation. Claims fold into one prompt per batch, and each prompt is debounced — polite to the provider. Its intake is the graph tier&#39;s fuzzy residue (pre-bucketed candidate pairs), not the raw claim set.</li></ul><p>Style opinions are never emitted at all.</p><h2 id="the-evidence-bar" tabindex="-1">The evidence bar <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-evidence-bar" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The evidence bar&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every violation is <code>claim + evidence + fix(optional)</code>. If a checker cannot cite its proof, it emits nothing — false positives kill a critic faster than false negatives ever could. This is why <code>command</code> has an allowlist, why a bad rule regex is silence + a doctor complaint, and why the semantic tier exists separately from the gate.</p><h2 id="sources" tabindex="-1">Sources <a class="header-anchor" href="#sources" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sources&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>[sources]</code> in <code>mimus.toml</code>: <code>docs</code> (glob list), <code>comments</code> (code comments — TODOs, claims in <code>///</code>), and <code>cards</code> — the kuda artifacts get audited as claims too, closing the loop: kuda remembers, mimus doubts, even about the memory itself.</p>',18)])])}const p=t(d,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,p as default};
